name: pipes and counting
boss: PipeWrench
flavor: Nothing here stands alone. It all flows through me.
brief: A pipe wires one command's stdout into the next one's stdin. That is
brief: the whole idea, and it is why this system needs so few programs: you
brief: are not given a tool for every job, you are given parts to build one.
brief:
brief: sort orders, uniq collapses neighbours, wc counts. uniq only sees the
brief: line before it, so it can only remove duplicates that are already
brief: adjacent -- which is why sort comes first, every time.
---
q: {%count% the lines in @log@|how many lines does @log@ have?|how long is @log@ in lines?}
a: wc -l < @log@
a: wc -l @log@
out: ^[0-9]+
ref: wc
teach: wc -l
hint: l for lines, same flag whether you pipe it in or pass a filename
---
q: {%count% the words in @txt@|how many words are in @txt@?|word count of @txt@}
a: wc -w < @txt@
a: wc -w @txt@
out: [0-9]
ref: wc
teach: wc -w
hint: words are whitespace-separated, not lines
---
q: {%count% the bytes in @txt@|how many bytes is @txt@?|the size of @txt@ in bytes}
a: wc -c < @txt@
a: wc -c @txt@
out: [0-9]
ref: wc
teach: wc -c
hint: c counts bytes -- pair with -m for characters on multibyte text
---
q: {%count% how many entries are in the current directory|how many things are in this directory?|number of files and folders here}
a: ls | wc -l
out: [0-9]
cmp: ref
ref: wc
teach: wc -l
hint: pipe a listing into wc -l to turn it into a count
---
q: {sort the lines of @txt@ alphabetically|put @txt@ in alphabetical order|order @txt@ a to z}
a: sort @txt@
out: ^ask why the fan
ref: sort
teach: sort
hint: no flags -- sort defaults to alphabetical, ascending
---
q: {sort @txt@ in reverse alphabetical order|order @txt@ z to a|sort @txt@ backwards}
a: sort -r @txt@
out: ^verify the root hash
ref: sort
teach: sort -r
hint: r flips the order
---
q: %show% the lines of @txt@ with duplicates removed, using one command
a: sort -u @txt@
out: ask why the fan
ref: sort
teach: sort -u
hint: u does the sort and the dedup in a single pass
---
q: %show% the lines of @txt@ with duplicates removed, using sort and uniq
a: sort @txt@ | uniq
out: ask why the fan
ref: uniq
teach: uniq
hint: uniq only collapses ADJACENT duplicates, so sort has to go first
---
q: {%show% each distinct line of @txt@ with how many times it appears|for @txt@, how often does each line occur?|tally the repeats in @txt@}
a: sort @txt@ | uniq -c
out: 2 rotate the logs
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -c
hint: c prefixes each line with how many times it appeared
---
q: %show% only the lines of @txt@ that appear more than once
a: sort @txt@ | uniq -d
out: rotate the logs
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -d
hint: d shows only lines that had a duplicate
---
q: %show% only the lines of @txt@ that appear exactly once
a: sort @txt@ | uniq -u
out: ask why the fan
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -u
hint: u here shows only lines that had no duplicate
---
q: {sort @csv@ by its third comma-separated field|order @csv@ using its third comma-separated field as the key|sort @csv@ on the third field, splitting on commas}
a: sort -t, -k3 @csv@
out: down
ref: sort
teach: sort -t
hint: -t sets the separator, -k picks the field
---
q: {sort @csv@ by the fourth field, numerically, largest first|order @csv@ by its fourth field, numeric and descending|sort @csv@ on field four, largest number first}
a: sort -t, -k4 -rn @csv@
a: sort -t, -k4rn @csv@
out: 42
ref: sort
teach: sort -k
hint: n makes the comparison numeric instead of text; r reverses it
---
q: {sort @txt@ ignoring the difference between upper and lower case|sort @txt@ treating uppercase and lowercase as the same|sort @txt@ case-insensitively}
a: sort -f @txt@
out: ask why the fan
ref: sort
teach: sort -f
hint: f folds case, treating upper and lower as equal
---
q: {check whether @txt@ is already sorted, without printing it|verify @txt@ is already in sorted order, without printing anything|confirm @txt@'s lines are already sorted, silently}
a: sort -c @txt@
ref: sort
teach: sort -c
hint: it says nothing when sorted, and names the first bad line when not
---
q: {sort @csv@ by human-readable sizes like 2K and 1M, smallest first|order @csv@ by sizes such as 2K and 1M, smallest to largest|sort @csv@ so human-readable sizes like 2K and 1M compare correctly, ascending}
a: sort -h @csv@
out: .
ref: sort
teach: sort -h
hint: h understands suffixes like K and M when comparing
---
q: {sort @txt@ ignoring unprintable characters|sort @txt@ as if the unprintable characters were not there|sort @txt@ skipping any character that does not print}
a: sort -i @txt@
out: .
ref: sort
teach: sort -i
hint: -i drops the bytes with no glyph before comparing -- case-blind sorting is -f's job
---
q: {sort @txt@ writing the result back over the file itself|sort @txt@ and write the sorted result to a named output file|sort @txt@, saving the output rather than printing it}
a: sort -o sorted.txt @txt@
state: exists:sorted.txt
ref: sort
teach: sort -o
hint: -o names the output file -- it can even be the same file you're sorting
---
q: {sort a list of version numbers so 1.10 comes after 1.9|sort version numbers so 1.10 lands after 1.9, not before it|order a list of version numbers correctly, so 1.10 follows 1.9}
a: sort -V @txt@
out: .
ref: sort
teach: sort -V
hint: V understands version numbers, so 1.9 sorts before 1.10
---
q: {collapse duplicate lines ignoring case|collapse duplicates, treating case as irrelevant|remove case-insensitive duplicate lines}
a: sort -f @txt@ | uniq -i
out: .
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -i
hint: sort -f groups case-insensitively, uniq -i then collapses it
---
q: {collapse duplicates comparing only the first 6 characters of each line|collapse duplicate lines using only their first 6 characters to compare|remove duplicates, comparing just the first 6 characters of each line}
a: sort @txt@ | uniq -w 6
out: .
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -w
hint: w limits the comparison to the first N characters of each line
---
q: %find% the length of the longest line in @log@
a: wc -L @log@
out: [0-9]
cmp: ref
ref: wc
teach: wc -L
hint: capital L -- reports the longest line's length, not a count of lines
